strategy|overall-plan|a plan for how to achieve an aim or organize actions|countable noun; strategy for doing something|A|Oxford and English Vocab both support a plan for action; use one broad noun boundary and compose a new example rather than reuse either source example.
thursday|day-of-week|the day after Wednesday and before Friday|proper noun; on Thursday; every Thursday|A|Oxford examples place Thursday in recurring calendar expressions; this is the standard day-name meaning and the proper noun keeps its initial capital in running text.
shocked|strongly-surprised-or-upset|feeling very surprised or upset because of something unexpected|adjective; be shocked by or to hear something|A|Oxford supports sudden news and an unexpected rent increase, so one adjective meaning covers both triggers without inventing another boundary.
everywhere|in-all-places|in or to all places, or in many places without exception|adverb; follows a movement or search verb, or modifies a clause|A|Oxford uses everywhere for a search and for broad distribution; both illustrate the same all-places adverbial meaning.
store|shop-selling-goods|a shop or other place where goods are sold|countable noun; at a store; a local store|A|Oxford and English Vocab both describe a place where people buy things; preserve the noun separately from the verb because their parts of speech and meanings differ.
store|keep-for-later-use|to put something in a place so it can be kept and used later|transitive verb; store something in a place|B|Oxford supplies the verb and a storage location while the paired source supports the store headword as a verb; this is distinct from the shop noun.
anthem|formal-or-religious-song|a formal song associated with a country or group, or a religious song|countable noun; national anthem; sing or perform an anthem|A|English Vocab describes a formal or religious song and its example identifies a national anthem; keep this supported song meaning without extending it to unrelated figurative uses.
solo|done-alone|done by one person alone, without other people taking part or helping|adjective; solo before a noun; a solo journey or performance|A|Oxford’s solo flight and English Vocab’s alone-without-help gloss support one adjective boundary for an activity done alone.
solo|musical-passage|a part of a musical performance for one voice or instrument alone|countable noun; perform or play a solo|B|Oxford identifies a guitarist’s short solo as a separate noun use; retain the musical passage apart from the adjective meaning alone.
insect|six-legged-small-animal|a small animal with six legs and a body divided into three main parts|countable noun; an insect; insects on or near something|A|Oxford examples describe an insect feeding on leaves and landing on clothing; they support the ordinary biological noun without adding unsupported categories.
severely|to-a-serious-degree|to a very serious or damaging degree|adverb; severely modifies a verb or participle|A|Oxford links severely with storm damage and drought effects, while English Vocab glosses it as very badly or seriously; these are one degree-adverb meaning.
mere|only-a-small-amount|used to emphasize that an amount is small or that something is unimportant|adjective or determiner; mere before a noun or quantity|A|Oxford’s ten-minute example emphasizes a small amount and English Vocab explicitly notes smallness or lack of importance; one boundary covers both related emphatic uses.
transparency|openness-and-clarity|the quality of being open and clear so that actions, spending, or an argument can be understood|uncountable noun; transparency in or of a process|A|Oxford’s spending example supports openness and English Vocab supports ease of understanding a situation or argument; combine these closely related abstract uses.
transparency|letting-light-through|the quality of a material that lets light pass through it so objects can be seen|uncountable noun; transparency of a material|B|Oxford separately illustrates a material suitable for windows because it is transparent; this physical property differs from openness of information.
campus|school-or-university-grounds|the buildings and surrounding grounds belonging to a school, college, or university|countable noun; on campus; the centre of a campus|A|Oxford’s bicycle and library examples locate activity within school grounds; use one institutional-place noun meaning.
surgical|connected-with-surgery|used in or connected with an operation or medical treatment involving surgery|adjective; surgical before a medical noun|A|Oxford’s treatment example and English Vocab’s definition both directly support the medical adjective sense.
surgical|precise-and-selective|very precise and limited in scope, especially when making cuts or reductions|figurative adjective; surgical cuts to a budget or plan|B|Oxford explicitly uses surgical for selective cuts to spending; retain this figurative extension separately from medical use and keep the example nonmedical.
colonial|connected-with-colonies|connected with a colony or the period when one country controlled another territory|adjective; colonial before history, rule, or buildings|A|English Vocab defines the relation to a country controlling another and Oxford supplies history and buildings; the examples fit one historically grounded adjective.
lay|put-flat-or-horizontal|to put or place a person or thing in a flat or horizontal position|transitive verb; lay something or someone on a surface; past laid|A|Oxford examples and English Vocab directly support placing something horizontally; keep this transitive meaning distinct from the egg and covering uses.
lay|produce-eggs|when a bird produces an egg|transitive verb; a hen lays eggs; past laid|B|Oxford gives hens laying eggs as a separate verb meaning; do not merge biological production with placing an object.
lay|place-a-covering|to place tiles or similar pieces over a surface to form a covering|transitive verb; lay tiles or a covering on a surface; past laid|B|Oxford explicitly shows workers laying tiles on a floor, which is a distinct surface-covering construction rather than simply putting one object down.
unfair|not-just-or-equal|not treating people in a fair or equal way, or not based on justice|adjective; unfair to someone; unfair of someone to act|A|Oxford’s blame and rule examples show unjust treatment; keep one broad adjective meaning rather than splitting the causes of unfairness.
configure|set-up-and-arrange|to set up a device or system by arranging its settings or components|transitive verb; configure a device or system for a purpose|A|English Vocab explicitly defines configure as setting something up and arranging it; retain one supported transitive verb sense.
immigration|process-of-settling-in-another-country|the process of moving to a different country from one's country of birth to live there permanently|usually uncountable noun; immigration to a country; rules or history of immigration|A|Oxford supports rules and history of immigration and English Vocab defines the permanent-settlement process; these contexts support the process meaning.
immigration|number-of-arriving-settlers|the number of people who move to another country to live there permanently|usually uncountable noun for the total; levels or amount of immigration|B|English Vocab explicitly distinguishes the number of people who immigrate from the process; preserve that count-related reading as a separate candidate despite Oxford's less explicit examples.
acceptance|receiving-or-approving|the act or willingness of agreeing to receive or approve an offer, person, or difference|usually uncountable noun; acceptance of an offer or difference|A|Oxford supports acceptance of a job offer and of cultural differences; these examples share an act or attitude of receiving or approving.
acceptance|agreement-that-claim-is-true|agreement that an idea, statement, or explanation is correct or true|usually uncountable noun; acceptance that a claim is true|B|English Vocab explicitly defines acceptance of a claim as agreement that it is right or true; keep this proposition-focused use apart from receiving an offer or accepting differences.
animation|animated-film-or-work|a film or other finished work made from images that appear to move|countable noun; an animation; watch or make an animation|A|Oxford identifies an animation watched by children as a finished work; English Vocab’s process meaning is recorded separately.
animation|process-of-making-moving-images|the process or technique of making images appear to move in a film, video, or computer game|usually uncountable noun; computer animation; animation of images|B|Oxford’s computer-animation example and English Vocab’s process definition support the production technique; distinguish it from the finished film.